id: design.retry-backoff
kind: design
title: Retry with exponential backoff
aliases: retry, backoff, jitter, exponential backoff
tags: reliability, resilience
questions:
  - when should a service retry a failed call?
  - what happens when the upstream starts returning 429?
invariant: Retry transient failures only, with exponential delay plus jitter, a bounded attempt count and a deadline shared with the caller.
answer_30s: Retry transient, idempotent operations with exponential backoff and jitter, always under an attempt cap and a total deadline, and never for errors that will not change.
answer_2m: |
  The reasoning for each ingredient: idempotency because a timeout may mean
  success; backoff because retrying immediately amplifies the load that caused
  the failure; jitter because synchronous clients form a thundering herd on the
  same schedule; a cap and deadline because an unbounded retry converts a slow
  dependency into leaked connections and stuck requests; classification because
  retrying a 400 wastes the budget and retrying a 429 requires a longer delay
  than a 500. Watch for layered retries: client retry times gateway retry times
  queue redelivery multiplies attempts and can keep a struggling dependency down.
  When the failure is sustained rather than transient, retries are the wrong
  tool and a circuit breaker or load shedding is the right one.
when_to_use:
  - transient network failures, brief dependency restarts, optimistic-concurrency conflicts
avoid_when:
  - non-idempotent writes, validation or auth errors, sustained dependency failure
failure_modes:
  - no jitter, so all clients retry simultaneously
  - retries without idempotency, creating duplicated side effects
  - retries at multiple layers with no global attempt budget
  - a retry storm that keeps the dependency overloaded (the case for a circuit breaker)
related:
  - design.idempotency
  - design.circuit-breaker
  - design.timeouts
  - rust.retry
  - k8s.reconciliation
sources:
  - https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter/
